reads unless we also block j. The hypothesis in parent said reduces main-memory traffic from O(N^2) to O(N^2/BLOCK) but that's wrong: scanning all j per tile still reads each j for every tile, so total j accesses = n * ceil(n/TI), same as original (each i reads all j). Tiling only improves cache reuse of p[i] across j? Actually within a tile, we read p[i] once and use it for all j; but we also read p[j] repeatedly. The inner loop over t uses p[i] from array each time; compiler may keep in registers if unrolled. But memory traffic is still O(n^2) because each pair (i,j) accessed once, which is inherent.\n\nThe best speedup likely comes from OpenMP parallelization across 4 cores plus SoA for better vectorization/cache line utilization. The baseline sequential time 0.671s; with 4 cores ideal ~0.168s. Best observed 0.155s slightly superlinear due to cache effects. So we need a version that achieves near 0.16s.\n\nLet's craft an experiment: Convert Body array into separate float arrays x,y,z,vx,vy,vz (SoA). Use OpenMP parallel for over i with static scheduling. For each i, accumulate force in scalar fx,fy,fz by looping j sequentially. This preserves exact per-body summation order. To improve memory locality, we can also use #pragma omp simd? But that might vectorize across j? No, accumulation is reduction on scalars; compiler may not vectorize due to dependency. We could instead process multiple i's together using SIMD across i (like tile of 4 or 8) while still each body's j-loop sequential. That would allow vector loads for p[i] and maybe improve ILP. But need preserve exact FP: if we compute dx,dy,dz per component with same operations but in different order? For a given body i, the accumulation over j is scalar; using SIMD across multiple i doesn't change each individual sum's operation order because each lane accumulates independently. So it's safe.\n\nWe can implement a tile of e.g., 4 bodies (or 8) processed together: for each j, load p[j] once, compute dx_t etc for